{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation**\n\n- `AssessmentSources::prepare` (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, lines 45–194) carries the `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- It resolves the subject from the loaded definitions, accepting requirements and named examples and rejecting terms.\n- It seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`, so the subject's own declaration is always the first mandatory input. This holds for examples as well as requirements.\n- `source_input` resolves that selector through `InputSelector::Resolve` (`src/inputs.rs`).\n  - That path includes every non-term definition, so examples are covered.\n  - The resolved text is the declaration's `raw_lines`, which keeps the opening `:::{example} …` line and the closing `:::` fence.\n  - `to_model_input` and `to_input` emit `requirements`, `matches` and `text` for that selector.\n- `collect_definitions` (`src/evidence.rs`) builds `raw_lines` from the whole block, `block.start..block.end`. It strips only the `:impl:` and `:test:` option lines.\n  - `md/reference.md` says option lines are not part of the definition text, and `requirements.references.anchors` makes them separate anchor inputs. `prepare` adds those through the anchor loop.\n  - So \"complete fenced declaration\" is satisfied under the project's own definition of a declaration.\n- This `prepare` pushes the subject selector and never filters it out. `generated_evidence_selector` returns false for `Requirements` selectors, so the declaration cannot be dropped as generated evidence.\n- Deduplication, the unresolvable-input errors raised before inference, and the evidence-omission rules belong to `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`, which `prepare` also implements.\n- `prepare` is the only place that builds this input. No other code implements this obligation without a marker, and I found no unspecified behaviour in the `{impl}` code.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` (line 494) carries the `{test}`.\n  - It calls `prepare` with `requirements.case`, a named example.\n  - It asserts `subject_kind` is `AssessmentKind::Definition(DefinitionKind::Example)`.\n  - It asserts `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's own declaration is the first mandatory input.\n  - The same test checks requirement subjects, cited context, and that earlier evidence inputs are not carried forward.\n- Other tests support the \"complete fenced text\" part:\n  - `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` asserts that the resolved text equals the declaration's `raw_lines`, including both fences.\n  - `requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order` stores a complete fenced example declaration.\n  - `cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates` does the same for an example.\n- One gap: the example case in the bundle test does not assert the fence lines in its text. That is covered indirectly by the tests above, and the behaviour is simple, so I don't count it as a defect.\n\nThe implementation and tests satisfy the requirement.",
  "references": "b899630e41f440f0f3cdb55b167fbf32959bdbf515b06e0008b680021fa1d4d4",
